\description{
Admission decisions for the 3,593 applicants to five departments of the
graduate school of the University of California, Berkeley, in fall 1973,
one row per applicant. Departments 1 to 5 are departments B to F of the
admissions table in base R's \code{datasets::UCBAdmissions}, which identifies
departments only by letter; department A is not included. The data are a
classic example of confounding and Simpson's paradox in logistic
regression.
}
\details{
\strong{Departments.} \code{department} is a code: 1 = B, 2 = C, 3 = D, 4 = E and
5 = F. Department A (933 applicants) is absent, so the data hold 3,593 of
the 4,526 applicants in \code{datasets::UCBAdmissions}. Use
\code{factor(department)} in models: entered as a number, the code would force
a straight-line trend across arbitrary labels.

\strong{One row per applicant.} The rows were expanded from a table of counts,
so there are only 20 distinct rows (5 departments x 2 genders x 2
decisions). \code{female} is 1 for women and 0 for men, whereas \link{berk_sub}
codes \code{male}. No value is missing, and the values are unchanged in version
1.0.0.

\strong{Simpson's paradox.} Overall, 27\% of women and 37\% of men were admitted
(odds ratio for women 0.64). But almost all women (1,702 of 1,727) applied
to departments C to F, which admitted 6\% to 35\% of their applicants, while
30\% of men applied to department B, which admitted 63\%. Controlling for
department, the odds ratio for women is about 0.97.

\strong{Suggested analyses.}
\itemize{
\item Logistic regression of \code{admitted} on \code{female}, without and with
\code{factor(department)}: how adjusting for a confounder changes the gender
coefficient.
\item Adding \code{female:factor(department)} gives the saturated model for the 10
department-by-gender cells. A likelihood-ratio test against it
(\code{anova(..., test = "Chisq")}) asks whether the gender effect differs
across departments, and is a goodness-of-fit test of the main-effects
model.
}
}
